 This is an absolute value problem. The general approach to solving an equation involving absolute values is to consider the cases where the expression inside the absolute value is positive or negative. Here, we distribute the absolute value by performing the two different cases:<Tags: think: thinking process}
\begin{align*}
x-3=2x+4\;\;\text{ or }\;\;-(x-3)&=2x+4          \\
x-3=2x+4\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-x+3&=2x+4&&\blue{\text{ getting }0 \text{ in each side of the equation}}\\

x-2x&=4+3 \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-x-2x&=4-3&&\blue{ \text{ The like terms are gathered}}\\
-x&=7 \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-3x&=1&&\blue{\text{ All the coefficients are brought to one side of the equations}}\\
\frac{-x}{-1}&=\frac{7}{-1} \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\; \frac{-3x}{-3}&=\frac{1}{-3}&&\blue{\text{ Each side is divided by the coefficient of } x }\\
x&=−7 \;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\; x&=-\frac{1}{3}&&\blue{\text{ Solutions for } x}
\end{align*}

So $x$ can be $-7$ or $-\frac{1}{3}$
